[{"category":"section","location":"lasso/#The-lasso","page":"The lasso","text":"","title":"The lasso"},{"category":"section","location":"lasso/#Objective","page":"The lasso","text":"lasso(X, y, λ) minimizes, over β ∈ ℝᵖ,\n\nfrac12nlVert y - XbetarVert_2^2 + lambda lVert beta rVert_1 qquad n = textttsize(X 1)\n\nby coordinate descent from β = 0. The factor 1/(2n) is ProximalBase's LeastSquaresLoss (which is ½‖y - Xβ‖²) scaled by 1/n. With this normalization β = 0 is a minimizer in exact arithmetic if and only if λ ≥ λmax = maximum(abs, X' * y) / n. At λ = λmax itself the solver can return a coefficient of the order of eps(T) (seen with Float32), where λmax computed outside the solver differs from the solver's own correlations by rounding.","title":"Objective"},{"category":"section","location":"lasso/#Inputs","page":"The lasso","text":"X::Matrix{T} and y::Vector{T} with T equal to Float32 or Float64; the fit has the same T. Other array types and mixed element types throw an ArgumentError that names the supported types.\nλ::Real, finite, nonnegative and representable in T (not overflowing to Inf and not underflowing to 0 from a nonzero value). It reaches the solver in its own type, so an Int, a Rational or a BigFloat level that T represents is accepted, and the stored fit.λ is T(λ).\nsize(X, 1) ≥ 1 is checked by this package. The remaining checks (a response of length n, p ≥ 1, finite entries of X and y, the level λ) are those of ProximalBase and ProximalSolvers; their errors are passed on unchanged.\nX and y are not modified and the fit keeps no reference to them.","title":"Inputs"},{"category":"section","location":"lasso/#Result","page":"The lasso","text":"using HighDimensionalModels, Random\n\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\nn, p = 100, 20\nX = randn(rng, n, p)\ny = X[:, 1:3] * [2.0, -1.5, 1.0] + 0.5 * randn(rng, n)\n\nsolver = CDSolver(order = :cyclic, momentum = false)\nfit = lasso(X, y, 0.1, solver)\nfit\nLassoSolution: 20 coefficients, 5 nonzero, λ = 0.1, 100 observations;\n  coefficient-change test of the solver (not an optimality certificate): met after 10 passes\n\nLassoSolution has the fields coefficients, residuals (y - X * coefficients, computed once), λ and result, the ProximalSolvers result unchanged. coef(fit), predict(fit, Xnew) (Xnew * coef(fit)), residuals(fit) and nobs(fit) are the StatsAPI methods.","title":"Result"},{"category":"section","location":"lasso/#Convergence-and-optimality","page":"The lasso","text":"The result of the solver is fit.result, a ProximalSolvers.CDResults, with converged, passes, maxchange, tolerance and minimum. For this problem converged = true means that the largest change of a coefficient in a full pass, in the solver's scaling of the changes, was below tolerance (the CDSolver documentation of ProximalSolvers defines both); it is not an optimality certificate, and this package adds no convergence flag of its own. A run that reaches maxpasses without that test being met returns converged = false and ProximalSolvers warns; a run that meets the test within the cap, even after one pass, returns converged = true.\n\nTo check a fit without the solver's stopping rule, compare the correlations of the residuals with the penalty level: at a minimizer, |Xⱼ'r|/n ≤ λ for every column and Xⱼ'r/n = λ sign(βⱼ) where βⱼ ≠ 0, with r = y - Xβ. Rounding limits the accuracy that can be attained, but what a run achieved depends also on tol, maxpasses and the conditioning of X: a loose tol can report converged = true with large deviations. The deviations have to be measured, as below.\n\ng = X' * residuals(fit) / n\nS = findall(!iszero, coef(fit))\n(converged = fit.result.converged, tolerance = fit.result.tolerance,\n largest_excess = maximum(abs, g) - fit.λ,\n support_deviation = maximum(abs.(g[S] .- fit.λ .* sign.(coef(fit)[S]))))\n(converged = true, tolerance = 2.888760014036554e-7, largest_excess = 1.6804660302183194e-9, support_deviation = 5.234520997210446e-9)","title":"Convergence and optimality"},{"category":"section","location":"lasso/#The-solver-argument","page":"The lasso","text":"The optional fourth argument is a CDSolver from ProximalSolvers, with its options (maxpasses, tol, order, relative, momentum, rng). CDSolver() visits the coordinates in random order, drawn from a borrowed random number generator, by default the one of the task, which advances when the ordering draws from it (a design with one column needs no draw, and a solver with maxpasses = 0 draws nothing). Two default calls on a design with several columns need not return identical coefficients. CDSolver(order = :cyclic, momentum = false) visits the coordinates in a fixed order. tol is the threshold of the change test of a coefficient, not a bound on the distance to the minimizer (see the CDSolver documentation of ProximalSolvers for its meaning and for the limits of Float32).","title":"The solver argument"},{"category":"section","location":"lasso/#Reference","page":"The lasso","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"function","location":"lasso/#HighDimensionalModels.lasso","page":"The lasso","text":"lasso(X, y, λ, solver = CDSolver()) -> LassoSolution\n\nMinimize ‖y - Xβ‖² / (2n) + λ‖β‖₁ over β, with n = size(X, 1), by coordinate descent from β = 0. X must be a Matrix{T} and y a Vector{T}, with T equal to Float32 or Float64, and λ a Real; solver is a CDSolver. Other argument types throw an ArgumentError, and nothing is converted. X and y are not modified, and the solution keeps no reference to them.\n\nn ≥ 1 is checked here. The other checks (matching dimensions, p ≥ 1, finite data, λ finite, nonnegative and representable in T) are those of ProximalBase and ProximalSolvers, and their errors are not altered. λ reaches the penalty as given, in its own type.\n\nThe loss is ProximalBase's LeastSquaresLoss, scaled by 1/n; the penalty is NormL1(λ); the solve is ProximalSolvers.solve!.\n\nThe convergence report is ProximalSolvers' (fit.result.converged, passes, maxchange, tolerance, minimum). For this problem converged reports the coefficient-change test of the solver (the largest change of a coefficient in a full pass, in the solver's scaling of the changes, against tolerance), not a bound on the distance to the minimum or on the KKT conditions. To check a fit, compute maximum(abs, X' * residuals(fit)) / n against λ and, on the support, X[:, j]' * residuals(fit) / n against λ * sign(β[j]). Rounding limits the accuracy that can be attained; the accuracy a run achieved depends also on tol, maxpasses and the conditioning of X, and has to be measured.\n\nWith CDSolver() the coordinate order is random and is drawn from the solver's random number generator, by default the one of the task, which is borrowed and advances when the ordering draws from it (a design with one column needs no draw). CDSolver(order = :cyclic, momentum = false) gives a fixed coordinate order. maxpasses caps the passes; a run that reaches the cap without meeting the change test returns converged == false and ProximalSolvers warns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HighDimensionalModels.lasso"},{"category":"type","location":"lasso/#HighDimensionalModels.LassoSolution","page":"The lasso","text":"LassoSolution{T, R}\n\nThe fit returned by lasso, with fields\n\ncoefficients::Vector{T}: the coefficients, one per column of the design;\nresiduals::Vector{T}: y - X * coefficients, computed once at the returned point;\nλ::T: the penalty level, converted to T;\nresult::R: the ProximalSolvers result, unchanged. For lasso it is a ProximalSolvers.CDResults, whose documented field minimizer is the same vector as coefficients.\n\nFor this problem result.converged reports ProximalSolvers' coefficient-change test, not an optimality certificate; see lasso.\n\ncoef, predict, residuals and nobs from StatsAPI are defined for it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HighDimensionalModels.LassoSolution"},{"category":"section","location":"#HighDimensionalModels.jl","page":"Home","text":"HighDimensionalModels has one estimator, the Gaussian lasso at a single penalty level:\n\nmin_beta in mathbbR^p frac12nlVert y - XbetarVert_2^2 + lambda lVert beta rVert_1 \n\nIt is a thin layer. ProximalBase.jl owns the mathematics (the loss, the penalty and their numerical contracts) and ProximalSolvers.jl owns the solver (coordinate descent and its stopping rule). Both check their arguments. This package forms the problem, calls the solver and returns the coefficients, the residuals and the solver's result.\n\nusing HighDimensionalModels, Random\n\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\nn, p = 100, 20\nX = randn(rng, n, p)\ny = X[:, 1:3] * [2.0, -1.5, 1.0] + 0.5 * randn(rng, n)\n\nfit = lasso(X, y, 0.1, CDSolver(order = :cyclic, momentum = false))\nprintln(count(!iszero, coef(fit)), \" nonzero coefficients of \", p)\nprintln(round.(coef(fit)[1:5]; digits = 2))\n5 nonzero coefficients of 20\n[1.9, -1.35, 0.94, 0.0, 0.0]\n\nThe lasso page gives the inputs, the result, how convergence is reported and how to check a fit independently.","title":"HighDimensionalModels.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-is-not-supported","page":"Home","text":"Inputs other than a dense Matrix{Float32} or Matrix{Float64} with a Vector of the same element type: integer, sparse and view designs, matrix responses and mixed element types are refused with an ArgumentError; nothing is converted.\nAn intercept, standardization and observation weights.\nPenalty paths, screening, continuation and cross-validation.\nWeighted, adaptive, feasible, scaled and square-root lasso; the elastic net and nonconvex penalties; generalized linear models.\nGroup, sparse-group and multitask regression; quantile regression; SLOPE and knockoffs; structured and fused penalties; low-rank and sparse-plus-low-rank models; varying-coefficient models; post-selection inference.\nGeneral coordinate-descent interfaces, the compatibility API of earlier versions, and extensions for HiGHS, Knockoffs, ProximalAlgorithms and StatsModels.\n\nThe complete earlier package, with its documentation, examples and changelog, is preserved on the branch legacy/full and the tag archive/pre-minimal-core/master.","title":"What is not supported"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reference","page":"Home","text":"The docstrings of lasso and LassoSolution are on the lasso page. CDSolver is that of ProximalSolvers, re-exported. coef, predict, residuals and nobs are those of StatsAPI.jl, with methods for LassoSolution.","title":"Reference"},{"category":"module","location":"#HighDimensionalModels","page":"Home","text":"HighDimensionalModels\n\nThe Gaussian lasso at one penalty level, lasso, as a thin layer on ProximalBase (the loss and the penalty) and ProximalSolvers (coordinate descent).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HighDimensionalModels"}]
